<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- CONVENTIONS.md "Project reference graph": Application may reference Domain and BuildingBlocks only —
         BuildingBlocks is itself BCL-only, so this brings in Result<T>/Error/ErrorKind without
         making Mongo/Rebus/HotChocolate reachable. The Gateway has no Domain project of its own
         (ARCHITECTURE.md "Mapping to Clean Architecture's rings") — its read model lives here as plain projection records instead of
         an aggregate. -->
    <PackageReference Include="BuildingBlocks" Version="0.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Interactors are internal sealed and registered against the open generic IInteractor<,>
         (CONVENTIONS.md "Use cases" [AT]). The validators beside them are internal sealed too —
         as a fact of this codebase, not as something that section states; do not read the
         citation as covering them. Either way the composition root has to name the concrete
         types to register them, so AddGatewayInfrastructure would not compile without this.
         Production wiring required by "Use cases", not a testing decision: CONVENTIONS.md
         "Reaching an internal from a test" carves out exactly THIS grant — Application to its
         own Infrastructure, for the composition root — and says in terms that targeting a
         production assembly is not itself a reason. Do not cite this comment, or that carve-out,
         to add a production grant of any other shape (Domain to Infrastructure, Infrastructure
         to Host, one service to another): those are judged by the three options like anything
         else, and need the same written justification.

         GL-69 removed the Gateway.UnitTests grant that used to sit beside this one, because
         nothing in that suite referenced an internal of this project: with the grant deleted,
         a `dotnet clean` plus a non-incremental rebuild of Gateway.sln is green, and the whole of
         Gateway.UnitTests then RUNS green (62 passed, 0 failed, 2 skipped, 64 total —
         the two skips are DeferredRuleTests' own, unrelated and pre-existing). A compile alone
         would have been the weaker claim, since a test can reach an internal through a lambda or
         a generic and still be resolved differently; the suite was run for that reason.

         So the grant granted nothing, while reading like a precedent that a unit test may reach
         into Application whenever it likes. A grant is the last of three options, never the
         ambient default; whoever first needs one here adds it back with the reason
         CONVENTIONS.md "Reaching an internal from a test" asks for. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Gateway.Infrastructure" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
